  <title>Frames, boxes and lines</title>

  <p><gui>+ Frame</gui> adds a box. A frame is also how to draw a straight
  horizontal or vertical rule, and a solid black block. In ZPL it is
  <code>^GB</code>.</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Width</gui> and <gui>Height</gui></title>
      <p>The outside of the frame, in dots, 1 to 32000. Its handles change
      the box exactly as you drag it.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Thickness</gui></title>
      <p>The border, drawn inward from the outside edge, so a thicker border
      never makes the frame bigger: the box is always the outside. Once the
      thickness reaches half the shorter side, the borders meet in the
      middle and the frame is solid black; the thickness cannot go past
      that.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Corner Rounding</gui></title>
      <p>0 for square corners, up to 8, the roundest the printer draws. It is
      a share of the frame's shorter side rather than a size in dots: at 8
      the corners' radius is half the shorter side, at 4 a quarter. So the
      same rounding looks rounder on a small frame than on a large
      one.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Colour</gui></title>
      <p><gui>Black</gui> or <gui>White</gui>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Reverse</gui></title>
      <p>Turns whatever is already printed beneath the frame's border white,
      and the paper under it black, whatever the colour.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <section id="rule">
    <title>Draw a straight line</title>
    <p>A rule is a frame as thin as the line: for a horizontal line 3 dots
    thick, make a frame as wide as the line and 3 dots tall, with a
    thickness of 2 or more so it is solid. Type the height in the Properties
    panel, since a handle won't make a side smaller than 20 dots. After
    that, dragging the line's end handles lengthens or shortens it and
    leaves it 3 dots thick. A vertical line is the same, turned the other
    way: 3 dots wide and as tall as the line.</p>
    <p>For a slanting line, use a <link xref="diagonal">diagonal
    line</link>.</p>
  </section>

  <note>
    <p>A printer cannot print white: a <gui>White</gui> frame prints nothing
    on bare labels. It shows only where it lies over something black, and
    the canvas draws it that way.</p>
  </note>
</page>
